.TH MAN 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
man \- display manual pages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B man
.RB [ \-aw ]
.RI [ section ]
\fIpage\fR[\fB.\fIsection\fR]...
.br
.B man
.RB [ \-aw ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B man
shows manual pages.
For each operand it finds a page, formats it with
.BR nroff (1),
and displays the result through a pager.
It is an applet of
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
An operand that contains a slash or a backslash is a
.IR file ,
which
.B man
formats as a page without searching.
Any other operand is the name of a
.IR page ,
which
.B man
looks for in the manual directories.
A page is written in man(7) or mdoc(7) macros, and may be compressed.
.SS Finding pages
A manual directory holds page sources in subdirectories named
.BR man1 ,
.BR man2 ,
and so on, and may hold preformatted text pages in
.BR cat1 ,
.BR cat2 ,
and so on.
The page
.B xxd
in section 1 is the file
.I man1/xxd.1
in a manual directory, or
.IR cat1/xxd.1 :
the section is part of both the directory name and the file name.
.PP
.B man
searches these manual directories, in order:
.IP 1. 4
The elements of the
.B MANPATH
environment variable, separated by semicolons.
.IP 2. 4
The directories named by the
.B MANPATH
and
.B MANDATORY_MANPATH
lines of the configuration file.
.IP 3. 4
The kit's own pages, in
.I ../share/man
relative to the directory that holds
.IR busybox.exe .
.PP
Empty elements, and elements spelled the same as an earlier one, are
ignored.
A relative element is relative to the root of the system drive, not to
the current directory.
.PP
In each directory,
.B man
tries the sections in turn, and in each section a
.B cat
page before a
.B man
page.
The sections are
.BR 0p:1:1p:2:3:3p:4:5:6:7:8:9 ,
or the list in the
.B MANSECT
line of the configuration file.
.B man
displays the first page it finds, and then goes on to the next operand.
A cat page hides the man page of its section.
.PP
If the first operand is a section and another operand follows, only
that section is searched, for every operand.
A section here is any text whose first occurrence in the list of
sections ends at a colon or at the end of the list.
That includes a run such as
.BR 2:3 ,
which searches both, and the tail of a section:
.B man p foo
searches only section
.BR p ,
for
.IR manp/foo.p .
.B 0
is not a section, because it first occurs in
.BR 0p .
A
.I page
that ends in a dot and a section, as in
.BR printf.3 ,
is searched for in that section only, which takes precedence.
.PP
When no page is found, and the name contains
.BR \-w64\-mingw32\- ,
.B man
looks again for the part of the name after it, so that
.B x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-gcc
finds
.BR gcc (1).
.SS Links
A man page file, not a cat page, of at most 300 bytes whose first line is
.BR .so ,
a space, and a name is a link.
Later lines are ignored.
.B man
shows the page it names instead.
A name without a slash is relative to the directory that holds the link.
A name with a slash, such as
.IR man1/grep.1 ,
is relative to the directory above that one.
Trailing white space, including a carriage return, is ignored.
After 10 links in a chain,
.B man
formats the next link file as a page, and
.B nroff
follows its
.B .so
request.
When the target cannot be found, nothing is displayed, and the operand
counts as found.
.PP
A
.B .so
request inside a longer page is handled by
.BR nroff .
It resolves relative to the manual directory the page was found in, or
to the current directory for a
.IR file .
.SS Formatting and display
To show a page,
.B man
runs the
.B nroff
that is next to
.IR busybox.exe ,
in the manual directory where it found the page, or in the current
directory for a
.IR file .
The page is its standard input, and its options are
.BR \-mandoc ,
.BI \-T charset\fR,
.BI \-rLL= w n\fR,
and
.BI \-rLT= w n\fR.
Its output and error messages go to the pager.
.PP
The
.I charset
is
.B utf8
when the ANSI code page of the process is UTF-8, and
.B ascii
otherwise.
The line length
.I w
is the width of the display less 2, or the width itself when that is
10 or less.
The width is that of the console window of standard output, or else of
standard error.
When
.B COLUMNS
is set, its value is the width instead, or 80 if its numeric value is
not from 2 to 29999.
Without a console and without
.BR COLUMNS ,
the width is 80.
.PP
A cat page is not formatted: it goes to the pager as it is.
.PP
The pager is
.BR "less \-R" ,
unless the configuration file, or
.B MANPAGER
or
.BR PAGER ,
names another one.
It is run by
.BR sh ,
so it may be a command line with arguments.
When standard output is a console and
.B LESS
is not set,
.B man
sets it to
.B R
for the pager, so that a plain
.B less
shows bold and underlined text too.
.PP
When standard output is not a console, there is no pager.
.B man
sets
.B GROFF_NO_SGR
to
.BR 1 ,
so that
.B nroff
writes plain text with no escape sequences, and writes the pages one
after another to standard output.
.SS Configuration file
.B man
reads the first of
.IR /etc/man.config ,
.IR /etc/man.conf ,
and
.I /etc/man_db.conf
that exists, in the root of the system drive.
The kit installs none.
A line holds a keyword and the rest of the line as its value.
A
.B #
starts a comment, a backslash at the end of a line continues it, and
other lines are ignored.
The keywords are:
.TP
.BI "MANPATH " directories
Add manual directories, separated by semicolons.
.B MANDATORY_MANPATH
is a synonym.
.TP
.BI "MANSECT " sections
Replace the sections to search, as a colon-separated list.
.TP
.BI "DEFINE nroff " command
Replace the formatter, which is otherwise the kit's
.B nroff
with
.BR \-mandoc .
.B man
appends the
.B \-T
and
.B \-r
options to
.IR command .
.TP
.BI "DEFINE pager " command
Replace the pager.
.B MANPAGER
and
.B PAGER
still override it.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-aw ,
and must come before the operands.
.B \-\-
ends them.
.TP
.B \-a
Display every page found, in the order found, instead of only the first.
.TP
.B \-w
Print the path of each page file found, one per line, and do not
display it.
The path of a compressed page ends in its compression suffix, and that
of a link is the link file's own.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Variables that apply to every applet, such as
.BR BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_SYSTEMROOT
A drive, such as
.BR D: ,
to use instead of the system drive.
.B man
makes the root of that drive its working directory before it searches,
which is why relative
.B MANPATH
elements, and
.I /etc
for the configuration file, are found there.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The display width; see
.BR "Formatting and display" .
.TP
.B GROFF_NO_SGR
Set to
.B 1
for
.B nroff
when standard output is not a console.
.TP
.B LESS
Set to
.B R
when it is not set and standard output is a console.
.TP
.B MANPAGER
The pager, run by
.BR sh .
It overrides the configuration file, and takes precedence over
.BR PAGER .
A set but empty
.B MANPAGER
or
.B PAGER
still counts, and then nothing is displayed.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Manual directories to search first, separated by semicolons.
.TP
.B PAGER
The pager, when
.B MANPAGER
is not set.
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.B sh
finds a pager that is not an applet, and where
.B man
finds the decompressors
.B gunzip
and
.BR unzstd .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was found.
.TP
.B 1
Some operand was not found, a relative
.I file
does not exist,
.B gunzip
or
.B unzstd
is needed but not found, or the command line is invalid.
.PP
A page that is not found is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
man: no manual entry for \(aq\fIname\fR\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
and the remaining operands are still processed.
Errors in
.BR nroff ,
the pager, or a decompressor do not change the status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read a page, and a page from section 5:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ man gcc
$ man 5 gdbinit
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find out where a page is kept:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ man \-w gcc
C:/w64devkit/share/man/man1/gcc.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read the page for a tool by its prefixed name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ man x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-ld
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read a page that is not installed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ man ./mytool.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Look in two more directories of pages first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ MANPATH=\(aqD:/docs/man;D:/more/man\(aq man mytool
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save a page as plain text, formatted for a 100-column display:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ COLUMNS=100 man gcc >gcc.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the
.B man
of Unix systems:
.IP \(bu 2
The only options are
.B \-a
and
.BR \-w .
There is no search by keyword.
.IP \(bu 2
Manual directories are separated by semicolons, since colons occur in
drive letters.
.IP \(bu 2
Formatting is done by the kit's
.BR nroff (1)
alone, not by a pipeline of groff, tbl, and col.
.IP \(bu 2
Page names are matched by the file system, which usually does not
distinguish case on Windows, so
.B man GCC
shows the page for
.BR gcc .
.PP
A file operand may use slashes or backslashes.
A drive letter with no slash after it, as in
.IR c:doc/page.1 ,
starts from the current directory of that drive.
A file operand that does not begin with a drive letter and a slash, or
with a UNC prefix, is made absolute first, which fails if it does not
exist.
.B man
then stops at once, before it processes any operand, with status 1 and
the message
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
man: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
A missing file that begins with a drive letter and a slash, or with a
UNC prefix, is reported as a page that is not found.
.PP
Pages may be compressed.
For each page file,
.B man
tries the name with
.BR .lzma ,
.BR .xz ,
.BR .bz2 ,
and
.B .gz
appended, in that order, before the name itself.
A file is decompressed whatever its name when its first bytes identify
gzip, compress, bzip2, xz, or zstd data, and a name ending in
.B .lzma
is enough for lzma.
The decompressors are programs run by name:
.BR bunzip2 ,
.BR unxz ,
.BR unlzma ,
and
.B uncompress
are applets, and
.B gunzip
and
.B unzstd
come from
.BR PATH ;
see
.BR gzip (1)
and
.BR zstd (1).
When a page needs one of them and it is not on
.BR PATH ,
.B man
stops at once with status 1.
.PP
.B man
reads a man page file of 300 bytes or fewer within its own process to
look for a link, and for gzip and zstd data that fails: the decompressor
reports an error, and
.B man
exits at once with status 0, without showing the page or processing
later operands.
.PP
The width comes from the console window, not the screen buffer.
Escape sequences for bold and underlined text pass through
.BR "less \-R" ;
see
.BR less (1).
The busybox manifest asks Windows for a UTF-8 code page, which takes
effect on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
On older systems, such as Windows XP,
.B man
passes
.BR \-Tascii .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR nroff (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR zstd (1)
